  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nUpper low near Nova Scotia finally loosens its grip on the\nregion and exits into the northern Atlantic. A NW gradient will\nremain over the area with winds gusting 20-25 mph from late\nmorning into the afternoon. Rising heights and warmer air 850 mb\nwill bring highs into the mid to upper 70s with low 80s near the\ncoast thanks to downsloping NW winds. \n\nA cold front will sag into the area Monday night into Tuesday\nmorning bringing slight chances for a shower while much of the\narea will remain mostly dry. Broad high pressure along the East\nCoast will promote fair weather Tuesday with highs mainly in the\n70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nModels remain in good agreement that strengthening area of low\npressure will track over James Bay into northern Quebec late\nWednesday through Thursday. The leading warm front will bring\nincreasing clouds Wednesday and possibly some showers Wednesday\nafternoon. Low level winds will ramp up Wednesday evening with a\ncorridor of 40-50 kt SW winds at 900 mb translating across\nsouthern NH and the coast around midnight Wednesday night.\nSoundings continue to look stable near the coast while there may\nbe a brief period where winds could gust in excess of 30 mph\nacross southern NH.\n\nDeep moisture will advect into the region through Wednesday\nnight ahead of the trailing cold front. The timing of the\nfrontal passage will occur overnight and therefore limit\ninstability for storms. Thunderstorms that form upstream may\nreach portions of the CT Valley through northern NH before\ninstability completely wanes with thunder chances diminishing\nsouth of the mountains. Widespread showers look likely across\nmuch of the area late Wednesday night while overall rainfall\namounts will be around 0.25 inches outside of any convection.\n\nThere will be a drying trend Thursday morning as winds shift\nwesterly. A secondary cold front will cross Thursday afternoon \nbringing renewed rain chances. Strong winds aloft will remain\npresent over the area Thursday with some soundings showing\nmixing to 850 mb. This may allow for widespread gusts\napproaching 30 mph and some spots approaching 40 mph.\n\nThe secondary front will usher in a cooler air mass by Friday\nwith high pressure moving overhead Friday night. This will favor\nradiational cooling that could bring northern valleys into the\n30s for lows Friday night.",
